{"parentUuid":"81886031-d2f3-4168-b296-29dbacbae5fa","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQ91DiZATTarru67XNQU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_read_input_tokens":164786,"output_tokens":8813,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":212},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8813,"cache_read_input_tokens":164786,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":2267,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"79f871b2-3241-4f53-b15d-bae491eab85c","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChQ91DVfMzqpGnk7tXCcV","requestRef":"514922ba-94ca-433c-a118-3117e7be5f05","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"a2fcf0ad-3df2-4b02-9437-2e7be3e98e30","timestamp":"2026-11-25T18:43:01.854Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/misrouted-failures","sessionId":"b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04","version":"2.1.330","gitBranch":"claude/misrouted-failures","slug":"linear-finding-kay-of-b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"a2fcf0ad-3df2-4b02-9437-2e7be3e98e30","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQ91DiZATTarru67XNQU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TyYsmi8jGCx5X3kUnFasKk","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kenneth/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_cd3120f7-030a-4af8-a6d1-28ecae789f1a/misrouted-failures-simple-changes.md","content":"# The 17 simple changes to misrouted failures (2026-11-25)\n\nKenneth approved the 17 places that `misrouted-failures-simple-or-not.md` calls simple, each only after\nits failure has been made to happen (\"Yes, the 17\"). This file has one entry per place. Each changed\nplace says what happened before the change, the change itself, what happened after, which handler\nreports it, and whether a person can get there by doing something ordinary. Each place left untouched\nsays why.\n\n**Counts.** 6 places changed (3 in the app, 3 on the server). Two of them are changed only in part,\nbecause only some of their spots could be made to happen: billing, and the shipped printer profiles.\n11 places are left untouched (5 in the app, 6 on the server).\n\n**Where the work is.** Nothing is committed or pushed.\n- App: the worktree `/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/misrouted-failures`, branch\n  `claude/misrouted-failures`, uncommitted. Full diff: `misrouted-failures-simple-changes-app.patch` in\n  this folder.\n- Server: `/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise`, `web_ent/jusprin/`, uncommitted. Full diff:\n  `misrouted-failures-simple-changes-server.patch` in this folder.\n\n**How the failures were made to happen.**\n- App: a scratch copy of the built app with its own data folder. A local stand-in program answered in\n  place of Sentry and, where needed, in place of the AI service, and passed everything else to the\n  local server. Every Sentry report the app sent was counted there. Nothing went to a real Sentry\n  project, and nothing went to a real AI service.\n- Server: the local server over HTTP, signed in with a test account's token. The local server's\n  `SENTRY_DSN` is empty, so its 500s go nowhere.\n- Evidence folders (logs, page messages, the stand-in's received reports):\n  `/private/tmp/claude/claude-502/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin/b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04/scratchpad/live/runs/<name>-before` and `-after`.\n  The scratchpad can be cleared by a reboot.\n\n**Tests.**\n- Server: all of `jusprin.v2.tests` pass. Each new or changed test was also run on the old code and\n  fails there.\n- App: `agent_bridge_tests` (648 cases), `jusprin_profile_tests` (20) and `jusprin_tests` (200) all\n  pass.\n\n## The app\n\n### Sign out with no network — changed\n\n- **Before.** The local server was reached through a proxy, and the proxy was stopped before signing\n  out. The app was then sent the same message Preferences sends for \"Sign out\". The log said\n  \"Unexpected error while signing out of JusPrin: … revoking the token at …/o/revoke_token/ answered 0:\n  curl:Couldn't connect to server\", and 1 report reached the stand-in (run `signout-before`).\n- **Change** (`Agent/CloudSignInMachine.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n               .on_error(reported(\"signing out of JusPrin\", [url](std::string body, std::string error, unsigned status) {\n  +                // No answer, such as no network: the token is already\n  +                // forgotten on this computer, and nobody can act on it.\n  +                if (status == 0) {\n  +                    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(warning) << \"Signing out of JusPrin reached no server at \" << url << \": \" << error;\n  +                    return;\n  +                }\n                   // Forgotten on this computer, the token still works on the\n                   // server.\n                   throw RuntimeError(\"revoking the token at \" + url + \" answered \" + std::to_string(status) + \": \" + error +\n  ```\n- **After.** Same arrangement. One warning line, \"Signing out of JusPrin reached no server at …\", and\n  0 reports (run `signout-after`). The token is still gone from the app's settings file.\n- **Handler.** Any other failure still throws inside `reported(\"signing out of JusPrin\", …)`, which\n  reports it.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes: signing out with the network off. That is why no answer at all is now not\n  reported. Nothing is shown, as before, because there is nothing for the person to do. An answer the\n  server refuses is still ours and still reported.\n\n### Own key: the service is down or overloaded — changed\n\nThe change covers three spots: an error answer of 500 or above, an overload or server error named\ninside a reply, and the AI models list. It applies only to a profile with the person's own key. It\ndoes not apply to a JusPrin account, where the service is ours, or to a model on this computer.\n\n- **Before.** The app used an OpenRouter profile with a key, and its AI address was pointed at the\n  stand-in.\n  - A 502 with OpenRouter's own words, \"Your chosen model is down…\" (run `40-down-before`): the chat\n    showed \"Something went wrong on our side\", and 2 reports were sent.\n  - A reply that failed inside the stream with `server_error` / `provider_overloaded` (run\n    `40-overloaded-in-reply-before`): the same sentence, and 2 reports.\n  - The chat title request failing with 502 after a good reply (run `40-title-down-before`): the log\n    said \"Unexpected error while making a chat's title\", and 1 report was sent.\n  - The models list answering 503 (run `40-models-down-before`): 4 reports for 4 failed reads, 2 of\n    JusPrin's list and 2 of OpenRouter's.\n- **Change** (`Agent/ProviderAgent.cpp`, `Agent/ModelCatalogFetch.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n       if (expected)\n           return this->expected(std::move(*expected), answered, words);\n  +    // The service of the person's own key failing on its side: whoever runs\n  +    // it can fix it, and nobody here can.\n  +    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in && !m_config.profile.on_this_computer && event.status >= 500)\n  +        // TRN %1% is an AI provider, such as OpenAI.\n  +        return this->expected({\"network_error\", Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The AI request could not reach the %1% service.\")), m_config.profile.display_name), true},\n  +                              answered, words);\n       unexpected(answered, words);\n  ```\n  ```diff\n   std::optional<AgentError> ProviderAgent::in_reply_answer(const json& answer) const\n   {\n  -    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in)\n  +    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in) {\n  +        // The service of the person's own key overloaded or failing on its\n  +        // side, as Anthropic, OpenAI and OpenRouter name it inside a reply.\n  +        const ServiceSays says = service_says(answer);\n  +        if (!m_config.profile.on_this_computer &&\n  +            (says.type == \"overloaded_error\" || says.type == \"api_error\" || says.code == \"server_error\" ||\n  +             says.error_type == \"provider_overloaded\" || says.error_type == \"provider_unavailable\" || says.error_type == \"server\"))\n  +            // TRN %1% is an AI provider, such as OpenAI.\n  +            return AgentError{\"network_error\", Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The AI request could not reach the %1% service.\")), m_config.profile.display_name), true};\n           return provider_answer(0, answer);\n  +    }\n  ```\n  ```diff\n               fetch->done(std::move(fetch->result));\n  +            // The service of the person's own key failing on its side is for\n  +            // whoever runs it; the list is marked failed above.\n  +            if (!fetch->profile.account_sign_in && !fetch->profile.on_this_computer && status >= 500)\n  +                return;\n               if (const std::optional<std::string> fault = model_list_fault(status, body))\n                   throw std::runtime_error(*fault);\n  ```\n  The sentence is the one the app already shows when the service cannot be reached. It is an existing\n  msgid, so the catalogs do not change. The in-reply names come from each service's documentation:\n  Anthropic's `overloaded_error` and `api_error`, OpenAI's `server_error`, and OpenRouter's\n  `provider_overloaded`, `provider_unavailable` and `server`.\n- **After.** Same arrangements.\n  - The 502 and the in-reply failure: \"The AI request could not reach the OpenRouter service.\", with\n    the retry the app offers for that sentence, and 0 reports.\n  - The title: the chat stays untitled, and 0 reports.\n  - The models list: 2 reports, both for JusPrin's list, whose failure is ours. OpenRouter's 2 are\n    gone.\n- **Handler.** An error answer below 500 that names nothing the person can act on still goes to\n  `unexpected`, which throws. The throw ends in `AgentHost::pump_stream`, and `AgentWebView` reports\n  it. A JusPrin account's failures are unchanged and still reported. A models-list fault still throws\n  inside `reported(\"reading the AI models list\", …)`.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes, a person with their own key meets this whenever their service has an\n  outage. Nobody here can fix it, so it is not reported. The sentence the app already has tells them\n  the trouble is at that service.\n- **A repeating failure.** Each retry is one more answer of 500 or above, and none of them is reported\n  now. Before the change, each retry sent another report.\n- **Tests.** `tests/agent/test_provider_agent.cpp`: 500, 503 and 529 moved from the \"names nothing a\n  person can act on\" loop into the table of expected failures, as \"could not reach the Test Provider\n  service\". `tests/agent/test_anthropic_codec.cpp`: an in-reply `overloaded_error` now expects the\n  `network_error` failure, not a throw. These were changed tests, and the old assertions passed on the\n  old code, which is how I know the new ones fail on it. I did not run the new tests on an old build.\n\n### Printer profiles shipped with the app fail to install or read — changed in part\n\nThe place has three spots. Two are in `PrinterSetup/OrcaImporter.cpp`. They are left untouched\nbecause they are reached only by importing from an OrcaSlicer install, and I did not make them happen.\nThe third, the printer setup form, is changed.\n\n- **Before.** I made a scratch copy of the app and cut Creality's\n  `resources/profiles/Creality/filament/fdm_filament_common.json` to 200 bytes. On Home I opened the\n  printer column's add-printer chat, chose to set up a printer myself, and picked the Creality\n  Ender-3 V3 SE with a 0.4 nozzle (run `profiles-before`). The form said \"The printer profiles could\n  not be read: Failed loading configuration file …\", followed by the file's full path on this\n  computer. 0 reports were sent.\n- **Change** (`PrinterSetup/SetupOptionsLoader.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n       SetupOptions options;\n       PresetBundle bundle;\n  -    // A vendor that cannot be read is named, not thrown; its error is in the text.\n  +    // The profiles shipped with the app fail to read only on a broken\n  +    // install, which is ours. The loader logs each file's error, with its path.\n       std::vector<std::string> failed;\n  -    const std::string errors =\n  -        bundle.load_system_vendor_from_dir(profiles_directory, vendor_id, ForwardCompatibilitySubstitutionRule::EnableSilent, &failed).second;\n  -    if (!failed.empty()) {\n  -        // TRN %1% is the profile loader's own error, in English.\n  -        options.error = Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The printer profiles could not be read: %1%\")), errors);\n  -        return options;\n  -    }\n  +    bundle.load_system_vendor_from_dir(profiles_directory, vendor_id, ForwardCompatibilitySubstitutionRule::EnableSilent, &failed);\n  +    if (!failed.empty())\n  +        throw std::runtime_error(\"The shipped printer profiles of \" + vendor_id + \" could not be read\");\n  ```\n  The includes of `libslic3r/format.hpp` and `Translate.hpp` were removed with it, because nothing else\n  in the file used them.\n- **After.** Same broken copy, same steps (run `profiles-after`). 1 report: \"Unexpected error while\n  running the printer chat: std::runtime_error: The shipped printer profiles of Creality could not be\n  read\". It has no path in it. The log has one \"Unexpected error\" line, and the form shows no error\n  text of its own.\n- **Handler.** The printer panel's `reported(\"running the printer chat\", …)`.\n- **Ordinary person?** No. The person cannot change the files shipped inside the app, and a good\n  install reads them. Only a broken install, which is ours, reaches this.\n- **Test.** `tests/jusprin/test_setup_options.cpp`: a folder without the profiles now must throw\n  \"shipped printer profiles of Prusa\". It failed before the change because nothing threw.\n- **Follow-up, not done.** `SetupOptions::error` and the form's handling of it are now never set. Removing\n  them is a further deletion outside this place.\n\n### Password sign-in: any 400 is \"That password is wrong.\" — untouched\n\nI could not make a 400 that is ours happen. The server's password sign-in answered every attempt with\na 400 that is the person's. A password of only spaces got `required`, and a wrong password got the\ninvalid-password code. No answer from it reached the app's \"anything else\" branch.\n\n### JusPrin's model list — untouched\n\nThe local server's models list answers only 200, 401, 426 or 500, and it has no rate limit. The answer\nthe place is about cannot be produced without changing the server.\n\n### The local tool server's helper tells the outside AI client to open JusPrin when JusPrin is open — untouched\n\nThe failure needs the tool protocol to change between the helper and the app. The protocol has not\nchanged, so it could not be made to happen.\n\n### A damaged printer-facts file is emptied with no word — untouched\n\nThe handler check failed. One path to the file's read starts from `EVT_SLICE_STATUS_CHANGED`, and the\nnearest handler on that path is in an OrcaSlicer-owned file. By the rules of this task, the place is\nleft alone.\n\n### A printer-host connection test that throws — untouched\n\nNo host's connection test could be made to throw against the real hosts. The Moonraker and OctoPrint\nstand-ins that run in Docker each answered with a failure value, not a throw.\n\n## The server\n\n### The app sends something the server refuses — changed\n\nThe gateway's refusals, the print report's two refusals, and the web link's two refusals now raise,\nso Django answers 500 and reports it. Kenneth on 2026-11-15: \"Treat them as unexpected errors.\"\n\n- **Before** (local server over HTTP, with the test account's token):\n  - `POST /ent/jusprin/v2/responses` with the model `no-such-model` answered 400 `unknown_model` \"This\n    model is not available.\". The log had one info line ending \"failure: answered 400 unknown_model\".\n  - `POST print_reports` with an empty `gcode_hash`, or with `facts={`, answered 400 `invalid_request`.\n  - `POST web_link` with the body `{` or `[1]` answered 400 `invalid_request`, with a \"Bad Request\"\n    warning in the log.\n- **Change**:\n  ```diff\n           except GatewayError as e:\n  +            # A request the app sent that this server refuses is ours: the app\n  +            # is on the other end. Kenneth, 2026-11-15: \"Treat them as\n  +            # unexpected errors.\"\n  +            if e.code in ('invalid_request', 'unknown_model'):\n  +                raise\n               # The code only, never the message or details, which can hold the\n               # person's numbers.\n               facts['failure'] = f'answered {e.status} {e.code}'\n  ```\n  ```diff\n  -    try:\n  -        facts = json.loads(request.POST.get('facts', '{}'))\n  -    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +    # The app fills these in, so a report it sent that is refused here is ours.\n  +    facts = json.loads(request.POST.get('facts', '{}'))\n       if not gcode_hash or outcome not in dict(PrintReport.OUTCOMES) or not isinstance(facts, dict):\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +        raise ValueError(f'The app sent a print report with gcode_hash {gcode_hash!r}, outcome {outcome!r} '\n  +                         f'and facts of type {type(facts).__name__}')\n  ```\n  ```diff\n  -    try:\n  -        body = json.loads(request.body or b'{}')\n  -    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +    # The app writes this body, so one refused here is ours.\n  +    body = json.loads(request.body or b'{}')\n       if not isinstance(body, dict):\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +        raise ValueError(f'The app asked for a web link with a body of type {type(body).__name__}')\n  ```\n- **After.** Same requests.\n  - The gateway answered 500 `server_error` \"Something went wrong on our side\", and logged an error\n    with the traceback ending `GatewayError: unknown_model`.\n  - The print report answered 500, and the log shows \"ValueError: The app sent a print report with\n    gcode_hash '', outcome 'success' and facts of type dict\".\n  - The web link answered 500 with a `JSONDecodeError` for `{`, and \"ValueError: The app asked for a\n    web link with a body of type list\" for `[1]`. A good body still answered 200.\n- **Handler.** The gateway's `_handle`, which calls `capture_exception` and answers `server_error`.\n  For the two views it is Django's own error handling and `handler500`.\n- **Ordinary person?** No. The app writes every one of these requests. The model names come from the\n  list the server itself sends. The app already treats the server's error answers as ours, so what the\n  person sees does not change.\n- **Tests.** In `test_gateway.py` and `test_capture.py`, 12 assertions now expect 500 `server_error`,\n  and the 8 tests named `…_is_400` are renamed `…_is_ours`. `test_print_reports.py` and\n  `test_web_link.py` each have one test that now expects the error to be raised. All of them fail on\n  the old code.\n\n### Guesses in billing — changed in part\n\nThe place has three spots. Only the card lookup is changed.\n\n- **Card lookup.**\n  - **Before.** A Chargebee failure of any kind showed \"no card on file\". A \"not found\" from the real\n    test site, read through the server's own code, gave no card, as it should. A refusal of another\n    kind could not be produced against Chargebee without changing the site's keys. So the refusal was\n    made with Django's test client inside a rolled-back test, not over a socket. On the old code the\n    page still loaded, with no card shown.\n  - **Change** (`ledger/billing.py`):\n    ```diff\n         try:\n             card = chargebee.Customer.retrieve(customer_id(user), env=_site()).card\n    -    except chargebee.APIError:\n    +    except chargebee.InvalidRequestError as e:\n    +        # An account that paid without a customer on this site, such as a\n    +        # plan bought before it had its own, has no card here. Anything else\n    +        # Chargebee answers is ours.\n    +        if getattr(e, 'api_error_code', None) != 'resource_not_found':\n    +            raise\n             return None\n    ```\n  - **After.** \"Not found\" still gives no card. A 401 `api_authentication_failed` now raises out of\n    the Billing and usage page.\n  - **Handler.** Django's `handler500`.\n  - **Ordinary person?** Not finding the customer is ordinary for an account with no customer on\n    JusPrin 2's site, and it still means no card. Nothing else a person can do reaches the other\n    failures.\n  - **Tests** (`test_billing.py`): a new test, where a 401 from Chargebee must make the page raise. It\n    fails on the old code with \"APIError not raised\". The cancel test's last page load now patches the\n    card lookup, because it used to rely on the old catch-all.\n- **Account deletion with \"subscription not found\" — untouched.** No JusPrin 2 Chargebee key exists\n  locally, so the deletion's Chargebee call cannot be made against a real site.\n- **A report conflict in the database — untouched.** To produce it, a report would have to be stored,\n  and a report's photo goes to the shared store. That would leave a stray file on a shared system.\n\n### The browser session is gone at checkout, or on the way back from paying — changed\n\n- **Before.** Over HTTP on the local server, I signed in to Credits and plans with the app's link, read\n  the page's form token, and then opened the guest page, which signs the browser out. Pressing the\n  pack's button answered 302 to Credits and plans. Coming back from paying, at\n  `checkout/done?id=…`, also answered 302 to Credits and plans, with nothing said.\n- **Change** (`v2/web.py`):\n  ```diff\n       user = _user(request)\n       if user is None:\n  -        return HttpResponseRedirect(reverse('jusprin_v2_web_prices'))\n  +        # A tab left open past its sign-in: the person's. They open the page\n  +        # from JusPrin again, as for a link that expired.\n  +        logging.getLogger(__name__).info('JusPrin 2 checkout pressed in a browser no longer signed in')\n  +        return render(request, 'jusprin/v2/sign_in_result.html', {\n  +            'heading': _('This link has expired'),\n  +            'line': _('Go back to JusPrin and open the page again.'),\n  +        }, status=410)\n  ```\n  ```diff\n       user = _user(request)\n  -    bought = billing.finish_checkout(user, request.GET['id']) if user and request.GET.get('id') else None\n  +    if user is None:\n  +        # Back from paying in a browser no longer signed in: the person's. The\n  +        # webhook adds what they bought, which they see in JusPrin.\n  +        logging.getLogger(__name__).info('JusPrin 2 checkout came back to a browser no longer signed in')\n  +        return render(request, 'jusprin/v2/sign_in_result.html', {\n  +            'heading': _('This link has expired'),\n  +            'line': _('Go back to JusPrin and open the page again.'),\n  +        }, status=410)\n  +    bought = billing.finish_checkout(user, request.GET['id']) if request.GET.get('id') else None\n  ```\n  The page and both lines already exist and are used by the expired sign-in link, so there are no new\n  strings.\n- **After.** Same steps. Both answered 410 with the page \"This link has expired. Go back to JusPrin and\n  open the page again.\", and each logged its info line.\n- **Handler.** None needed. The failure is the person's, so it is handled with normal control flow and\n  not reported.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes: a tab left open past its sign-in, or signing out in another tab. They are\n  now told to open the page from JusPrin again.\n- **Test** (`test_billing.py`): a new test, where both addresses with no signed-in browser must give\n  410 and \"This link has expired\". It fails on the old code with \"302 != 410\".\n\n### A paid Chargebee event that matches nothing — untouched\n\nThe \"ordinary person\" check found that a person can get there. Deleting an account (`_delete_account`)\nleaves Chargebee's later events for that customer matching nothing. Raising there would report an\nordinary deletion, and Chargebee would retry the event again and again.\n\n### A wrong-scope or wrong-client token is told \"Sign in to JusPrin to continue.\" — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. The only tokens are the JusPrin client's \"read write\" tokens. Making a token for\nanother client or another scope would write to the shared database.\n\n### JusPrin 1: a cancelled checkout raises a 500 — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. JusPrin 1's checkout sets no cancel address, so cancelling never reaches this\ncallback.\n\n### JusPrin 1 checkout: a bare catch drops the Obico Pro discount — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. A Chargebee failure other than \"not found\" cannot be produced without changing the\nserver's Chargebee settings.\n\n### JusPrin 1 plan read: any failure answers a 500 with \"Failed to retrieve subscription plan\" — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. It needs the database to fail during the read, which cannot be arranged from\noutside the code on the shared local stack.\n\n### A report photo in HEIC, or the server failing to read the upload — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. An operating-system read failure of an uploaded file cannot be caused from\noutside. A HEIC photo is already the person's and is already told so, which is right.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_read_input_tokens":164786,"output_tokens":8813,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":212},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8813,"cache_read_input_tokens":164786,"cache_creation_input_tokens":628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":628},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":69578,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TyYsmi8jGCx5X3kUnFasKk":{"file_path":"/Users/kenneth/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_cd3120f7-030a-4af8-a6d1-28ecae789f1a/misrouted-failures-simple-changes.md","content":"# The 17 simple changes to misrouted failures (2026-11-25)\n\nKenneth approved the 17 places that `misrouted-failures-simple-or-not.md` calls simple, each only after\nits failure has been made to happen (\"Yes, the 17\"). This file has one entry per place. Each changed\nplace says what happened before the change, the change itself, what happened after, which handler\nreports it, and whether a person can get there by doing something ordinary. Each place left untouched\nsays why.\n\n**Counts.** 6 places changed (3 in the app, 3 on the server). Two of them are changed only in part,\nbecause only some of their spots could be made to happen: billing, and the shipped printer profiles.\n11 places are left untouched (5 in the app, 6 on the server).\n\n**Where the work is.** Nothing is committed or pushed.\n- App: the worktree `/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/misrouted-failures`, branch\n  `claude/misrouted-failures`, uncommitted. Full diff: `misrouted-failures-simple-changes-app.patch` in\n  this folder.\n- Server: `/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise`, `web_ent/jusprin/`, uncommitted. Full diff:\n  `misrouted-failures-simple-changes-server.patch` in this folder.\n\n**How the failures were made to happen.**\n- App: a scratch copy of the built app with its own data folder. A local stand-in program answered in\n  place of Sentry and, where needed, in place of the AI service, and passed everything else to the\n  local server. Every Sentry report the app sent was counted there. Nothing went to a real Sentry\n  project, and nothing went to a real AI service.\n- Server: the local server over HTTP, signed in with a test account's token. The local server's\n  `SENTRY_DSN` is empty, so its 500s go nowhere.\n- Evidence folders (logs, page messages, the stand-in's received reports):\n  `/private/tmp/claude/claude-502/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin/b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04/scratchpad/live/runs/<name>-before` and `-after`.\n  The scratchpad can be cleared by a reboot.\n\n**Tests.**\n- Server: all of `jusprin.v2.tests` pass. Each new or changed test was also run on the old code and\n  fails there.\n- App: `agent_bridge_tests` (648 cases), `jusprin_profile_tests` (20) and `jusprin_tests` (200) all\n  pass.\n\n## The app\n\n### Sign out with no network — changed\n\n- **Before.** The local server was reached through a proxy, and the proxy was stopped before signing\n  out. The app was then sent the same message Preferences sends for \"Sign out\". The log said\n  \"Unexpected error while signing out of JusPrin: … revoking the token at …/o/revoke_token/ answered 0:\n  curl:Couldn't connect to server\", and 1 report reached the stand-in (run `signout-before`).\n- **Change** (`Agent/CloudSignInMachine.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n               .on_error(reported(\"signing out of JusPrin\", [url](std::string body, std::string error, unsigned status) {\n  +                // No answer, such as no network: the token is already\n  +                // forgotten on this computer, and nobody can act on it.\n  +                if (status == 0) {\n  +                    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(warning) << \"Signing out of JusPrin reached no server at \" << url << \": \" << error;\n  +                    return;\n  +                }\n                   // Forgotten on this computer, the token still works on the\n                   // server.\n                   throw RuntimeError(\"revoking the token at \" + url + \" answered \" + std::to_string(status) + \": \" + error +\n  ```\n- **After.** Same arrangement. One warning line, \"Signing out of JusPrin reached no server at …\", and\n  0 reports (run `signout-after`). The token is still gone from the app's settings file.\n- **Handler.** Any other failure still throws inside `reported(\"signing out of JusPrin\", …)`, which\n  reports it.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes: signing out with the network off. That is why no answer at all is now not\n  reported. Nothing is shown, as before, because there is nothing for the person to do. An answer the\n  server refuses is still ours and still reported.\n\n### Own key: the service is down or overloaded — changed\n\nThe change covers three spots: an error answer of 500 or above, an overload or server error named\ninside a reply, and the AI models list. It applies only to a profile with the person's own key. It\ndoes not apply to a JusPrin account, where the service is ours, or to a model on this computer.\n\n- **Before.** The app used an OpenRouter profile with a key, and its AI address was pointed at the\n  stand-in.\n  - A 502 with OpenRouter's own words, \"Your chosen model is down…\" (run `40-down-before`): the chat\n    showed \"Something went wrong on our side\", and 2 reports were sent.\n  - A reply that failed inside the stream with `server_error` / `provider_overloaded` (run\n    `40-overloaded-in-reply-before`): the same sentence, and 2 reports.\n  - The chat title request failing with 502 after a good reply (run `40-title-down-before`): the log\n    said \"Unexpected error while making a chat's title\", and 1 report was sent.\n  - The models list answering 503 (run `40-models-down-before`): 4 reports for 4 failed reads, 2 of\n    JusPrin's list and 2 of OpenRouter's.\n- **Change** (`Agent/ProviderAgent.cpp`, `Agent/ModelCatalogFetch.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n       if (expected)\n           return this->expected(std::move(*expected), answered, words);\n  +    // The service of the person's own key failing on its side: whoever runs\n  +    // it can fix it, and nobody here can.\n  +    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in && !m_config.profile.on_this_computer && event.status >= 500)\n  +        // TRN %1% is an AI provider, such as OpenAI.\n  +        return this->expected({\"network_error\", Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The AI request could not reach the %1% service.\")), m_config.profile.display_name), true},\n  +                              answered, words);\n       unexpected(answered, words);\n  ```\n  ```diff\n   std::optional<AgentError> ProviderAgent::in_reply_answer(const json& answer) const\n   {\n  -    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in)\n  +    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in) {\n  +        // The service of the person's own key overloaded or failing on its\n  +        // side, as Anthropic, OpenAI and OpenRouter name it inside a reply.\n  +        const ServiceSays says = service_says(answer);\n  +        if (!m_config.profile.on_this_computer &&\n  +            (says.type == \"overloaded_error\" || says.type == \"api_error\" || says.code == \"server_error\" ||\n  +             says.error_type == \"provider_overloaded\" || says.error_type == \"provider_unavailable\" || says.error_type == \"server\"))\n  +            // TRN %1% is an AI provider, such as OpenAI.\n  +            return AgentError{\"network_error\", Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The AI request could not reach the %1% service.\")), m_config.profile.display_name), true};\n           return provider_answer(0, answer);\n  +    }\n  ```\n  ```diff\n               fetch->done(std::move(fetch->result));\n  +            // The service of the person's own key failing on its side is for\n  +            // whoever runs it; the list is marked failed above.\n  +            if (!fetch->profile.account_sign_in && !fetch->profile.on_this_computer && status >= 500)\n  +                return;\n               if (const std::optional<std::string> fault = model_list_fault(status, body))\n                   throw std::runtime_error(*fault);\n  ```\n  The sentence is the one the app already shows when the service cannot be reached. It is an existing\n  msgid, so the catalogs do not change. The in-reply names come from each service's documentation:\n  Anthropic's `overloaded_error` and `api_error`, OpenAI's `server_error`, and OpenRouter's\n  `provider_overloaded`, `provider_unavailable` and `server`.\n- **After.** Same arrangements.\n  - The 502 and the in-reply failure: \"The AI request could not reach the OpenRouter service.\", with\n    the retry the app offers for that sentence, and 0 reports.\n  - The title: the chat stays untitled, and 0 reports.\n  - The models list: 2 reports, both for JusPrin's list, whose failure is ours. OpenRouter's 2 are\n    gone.\n- **Handler.** An error answer below 500 that names nothing the person can act on still goes to\n  `unexpected`, which throws. The throw ends in `AgentHost::pump_stream`, and `AgentWebView` reports\n  it. A JusPrin account's failures are unchanged and still reported. A models-list fault still throws\n  inside `reported(\"reading the AI models list\", …)`.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes, a person with their own key meets this whenever their service has an\n  outage. Nobody here can fix it, so it is not reported. The sentence the app already has tells them\n  the trouble is at that service.\n- **A repeating failure.** Each retry is one more answer of 500 or above, and none of them is reported\n  now. Before the change, each retry sent another report.\n- **Tests.** `tests/agent/test_provider_agent.cpp`: 500, 503 and 529 moved from the \"names nothing a\n  person can act on\" loop into the table of expected failures, as \"could not reach the Test Provider\n  service\". `tests/agent/test_anthropic_codec.cpp`: an in-reply `overloaded_error` now expects the\n  `network_error` failure, not a throw. These were changed tests, and the old assertions passed on the\n  old code, which is how I know the new ones fail on it. I did not run the new tests on an old build.\n\n### Printer profiles shipped with the app fail to install or read — changed in part\n\nThe place has three spots. Two are in `PrinterSetup/OrcaImporter.cpp`. They are left untouched\nbecause they are reached only by importing from an OrcaSlicer install, and I did not make them happen.\nThe third, the printer setup form, is changed.\n\n- **Before.** I made a scratch copy of the app and cut Creality's\n  `resources/profiles/Creality/filament/fdm_filament_common.json` to 200 bytes. On Home I opened the\n  printer column's add-printer chat, chose to set up a printer myself, and picked the Creality\n  Ender-3 V3 SE with a 0.4 nozzle (run `profiles-before`). The form said \"The printer profiles could\n  not be read: Failed loading configuration file …\", followed by the file's full path on this\n  computer. 0 reports were sent.\n- **Change** (`PrinterSetup/SetupOptionsLoader.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n       SetupOptions options;\n       PresetBundle bundle;\n  -    // A vendor that cannot be read is named, not thrown; its error is in the text.\n  +    // The profiles shipped with the app fail to read only on a broken\n  +    // install, which is ours. The loader logs each file's error, with its path.\n       std::vector<std::string> failed;\n  -    const std::string errors =\n  -        bundle.load_system_vendor_from_dir(profiles_directory, vendor_id, ForwardCompatibilitySubstitutionRule::EnableSilent, &failed).second;\n  -    if (!failed.empty()) {\n  -        // TRN %1% is the profile loader's own error, in English.\n  -        options.error = Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The printer profiles could not be read: %1%\")), errors);\n  -        return options;\n  -    }\n  +    bundle.load_system_vendor_from_dir(profiles_directory, vendor_id, ForwardCompatibilitySubstitutionRule::EnableSilent, &failed);\n  +    if (!failed.empty())\n  +        throw std::runtime_error(\"The shipped printer profiles of \" + vendor_id + \" could not be read\");\n  ```\n  The includes of `libslic3r/format.hpp` and `Translate.hpp` were removed with it, because nothing else\n  in the file used them.\n- **After.** Same broken copy, same steps (run `profiles-after`). 1 report: \"Unexpected error while\n  running the printer chat: std::runtime_error: The shipped printer profiles of Creality could not be\n  read\". It has no path in it. The log has one \"Unexpected error\" line, and the form shows no error\n  text of its own.\n- **Handler.** The printer panel's `reported(\"running the printer chat\", …)`.\n- **Ordinary person?** No. The person cannot change the files shipped inside the app, and a good\n  install reads them. Only a broken install, which is ours, reaches this.\n- **Test.** `tests/jusprin/test_setup_options.cpp`: a folder without the profiles now must throw\n  \"shipped printer profiles of Prusa\". It failed before the change because nothing threw.\n- **Follow-up, not done.** `SetupOptions::error` and the form's handling of it are now never set. Removing\n  them is a further deletion outside this place.\n\n### Password sign-in: any 400 is \"That password is wrong.\" — untouched\n\nI could not make a 400 that is ours happen. The server's password sign-in answered every attempt with\na 400 that is the person's. A password of only spaces got `required`, and a wrong password got the\ninvalid-password code. No answer from it reached the app's \"anything else\" branch.\n\n### JusPrin's model list — untouched\n\nThe local server's models list answers only 200, 401, 426 or 500, and it has no rate limit. The answer\nthe place is about cannot be produced without changing the server.\n\n### The local tool server's helper tells the outside AI client to open JusPrin when JusPrin is open — untouched\n\nThe failure needs the tool protocol to change between the helper and the app. The protocol has not\nchanged, so it could not be made to happen.\n\n### A damaged printer-facts file is emptied with no word — untouched\n\nThe handler check failed. One path to the file's read starts from `EVT_SLICE_STATUS_CHANGED`, and the\nnearest handler on that path is in an OrcaSlicer-owned file. By the rules of this task, the place is\nleft alone.\n\n### A printer-host connection test that throws — untouched\n\nNo host's connection test could be made to throw against the real hosts. The Moonraker and OctoPrint\nstand-ins that run in Docker each answered with a failure value, not a throw.\n\n## The server\n\n### The app sends something the server refuses — changed\n\nThe gateway's refusals, the print report's two refusals, and the web link's two refusals now raise,\nso Django answers 500 and reports it. Kenneth on 2026-11-15: \"Treat them as unexpected errors.\"\n\n- **Before** (local server over HTTP, with the test account's token):\n  - `POST /ent/jusprin/v2/responses` with the model `no-such-model` answered 400 `unknown_model` \"This\n    model is not available.\". The log had one info line ending \"failure: answered 400 unknown_model\".\n  - `POST print_reports` with an empty `gcode_hash`, or with `facts={`, answered 400 `invalid_request`.\n  - `POST web_link` with the body `{` or `[1]` answered 400 `invalid_request`, with a \"Bad Request\"\n    warning in the log.\n- **Change**:\n  ```diff\n           except GatewayError as e:\n  +            # A request the app sent that this server refuses is ours: the app\n  +            # is on the other end. Kenneth, 2026-11-15: \"Treat them as\n  +            # unexpected errors.\"\n  +            if e.code in ('invalid_request', 'unknown_model'):\n  +                raise\n               # The code only, never the message or details, which can hold the\n               # person's numbers.\n               facts['failure'] = f'answered {e.status} {e.code}'\n  ```\n  ```diff\n  -    try:\n  -        facts = json.loads(request.POST.get('facts', '{}'))\n  -    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +    # The app fills these in, so a report it sent that is refused here is ours.\n  +    facts = json.loads(request.POST.get('facts', '{}'))\n       if not gcode_hash or outcome not in dict(PrintReport.OUTCOMES) or not isinstance(facts, dict):\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +        raise ValueError(f'The app sent a print report with gcode_hash {gcode_hash!r}, outcome {outcome!r} '\n  +                         f'and facts of type {type(facts).__name__}')\n  ```\n  ```diff\n  -    try:\n  -        body = json.loads(request.body or b'{}')\n  -    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +    # The app writes this body, so one refused here is ours.\n  +    body = json.loads(request.body or b'{}')\n       if not isinstance(body, dict):\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +        raise ValueError(f'The app asked for a web link with a body of type {type(body).__name__}')\n  ```\n- **After.** Same requests.\n  - The gateway answered 500 `server_error` \"Something went wrong on our side\", and logged an error\n    with the traceback ending `GatewayError: unknown_model`.\n  - The print report answered 500, and the log shows \"ValueError: The app sent a print report with\n    gcode_hash '', outcome 'success' and facts of type dict\".\n  - The web link answered 500 with a `JSONDecodeError` for `{`, and \"ValueError: The app asked for a\n    web link with a body of type list\" for `[1]`. A good body still answered 200.\n- **Handler.** The gateway's `_handle`, which calls `capture_exception` and answers `server_error`.\n  For the two views it is Django's own error handling and `handler500`.\n- **Ordinary person?** No. The app writes every one of these requests. The model names come from the\n  list the server itself sends. The app already treats the server's error answers as ours, so what the\n  person sees does not change.\n- **Tests.** In `test_gateway.py` and `test_capture.py`, 12 assertions now expect 500 `server_error`,\n  and the 8 tests named `…_is_400` are renamed `…_is_ours`. `test_print_reports.py` and\n  `test_web_link.py` each have one test that now expects the error to be raised. All of them fail on\n  the old code.\n\n### Guesses in billing — changed in part\n\nThe place has three spots. Only the card lookup is changed.\n\n- **Card lookup.**\n  - **Before.** A Chargebee failure of any kind showed \"no card on file\". A \"not found\" from the real\n    test site, read through the server's own code, gave no card, as it should. A refusal of another\n    kind could not be produced against Chargebee without changing the site's keys. So the refusal was\n    made with Django's test client inside a rolled-back test, not over a socket. On the old code the\n    page still loaded, with no card shown.\n  - **Change** (`ledger/billing.py`):\n    ```diff\n         try:\n             card = chargebee.Customer.retrieve(customer_id(user), env=_site()).card\n    -    except chargebee.APIError:\n    +    except chargebee.InvalidRequestError as e:\n    +        # An account that paid without a customer on this site, such as a\n    +        # plan bought before it had its own, has no card here. Anything else\n    +        # Chargebee answers is ours.\n    +        if getattr(e, 'api_error_code', None) != 'resource_not_found':\n    +            raise\n             return None\n    ```\n  - **After.** \"Not found\" still gives no card. A 401 `api_authentication_failed` now raises out of\n    the Billing and usage page.\n  - **Handler.** Django's `handler500`.\n  - **Ordinary person?** Not finding the customer is ordinary for an account with no customer on\n    JusPrin 2's site, and it still means no card. Nothing else a person can do reaches the other\n    failures.\n  - **Tests** (`test_billing.py`): a new test, where a 401 from Chargebee must make the page raise. It\n    fails on the old code with \"APIError not raised\". The cancel test's last page load now patches the\n    card lookup, because it used to rely on the old catch-all.\n- **Account deletion with \"subscription not found\" — untouched.** No JusPrin 2 Chargebee key exists\n  locally, so the deletion's Chargebee call cannot be made against a real site.\n- **A report conflict in the database — untouched.** To produce it, a report would have to be stored,\n  and a report's photo goes to the shared store. That would leave a stray file on a shared system.\n\n### The browser session is gone at checkout, or on the way back from paying — changed\n\n- **Before.** Over HTTP on the local server, I signed in to Credits and plans with the app's link, read\n  the page's form token, and then opened the guest page, which signs the browser out. Pressing the\n  pack's button answered 302 to Credits and plans. Coming back from paying, at\n  `checkout/done?id=…`, also answered 302 to Credits and plans, with nothing said.\n- **Change** (`v2/web.py`):\n  ```diff\n       user = _user(request)\n       if user is None:\n  -        return HttpResponseRedirect(reverse('jusprin_v2_web_prices'))\n  +        # A tab left open past its sign-in: the person's. They open the page\n  +        # from JusPrin again, as for a link that expired.\n  +        logging.getLogger(__name__).info('JusPrin 2 checkout pressed in a browser no longer signed in')\n  +        return render(request, 'jusprin/v2/sign_in_result.html', {\n  +            'heading': _('This link has expired'),\n  +            'line': _('Go back to JusPrin and open the page again.'),\n  +        }, status=410)\n  ```\n  ```diff\n       user = _user(request)\n  -    bought = billing.finish_checkout(user, request.GET['id']) if user and request.GET.get('id') else None\n  +    if user is None:\n  +        # Back from paying in a browser no longer signed in: the person's. The\n  +        # webhook adds what they bought, which they see in JusPrin.\n  +        logging.getLogger(__name__).info('JusPrin 2 checkout came back to a browser no longer signed in')\n  +        return render(request, 'jusprin/v2/sign_in_result.html', {\n  +            'heading': _('This link has expired'),\n  +            'line': _('Go back to JusPrin and open the page again.'),\n  +        }, status=410)\n  +    bought = billing.finish_checkout(user, request.GET['id']) if request.GET.get('id') else None\n  ```\n  The page and both lines already exist and are used by the expired sign-in link, so there are no new\n  strings.\n- **After.** Same steps. Both answered 410 with the page \"This link has expired. Go back to JusPrin and\n  open the page again.\", and each logged its info line.\n- **Handler.** None needed. The failure is the person's, so it is handled with normal control flow and\n  not reported.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes: a tab left open past its sign-in, or signing out in another tab. They are\n  now told to open the page from JusPrin again.\n- **Test** (`test_billing.py`): a new test, where both addresses with no signed-in browser must give\n  410 and \"This link has expired\". It fails on the old code with \"302 != 410\".\n\n### A paid Chargebee event that matches nothing — untouched\n\nThe \"ordinary person\" check found that a person can get there. Deleting an account (`_delete_account`)\nleaves Chargebee's later events for that customer matching nothing. Raising there would report an\nordinary deletion, and Chargebee would retry the event again and again.\n\n### A wrong-scope or wrong-client token is told \"Sign in to JusPrin to continue.\" — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. The only tokens are the JusPrin client's \"read write\" tokens. Making a token for\nanother client or another scope would write to the shared database.\n\n### JusPrin 1: a cancelled checkout raises a 500 — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. JusPrin 1's checkout sets no cancel address, so cancelling never reaches this\ncallback.\n\n### JusPrin 1 checkout: a bare catch drops the Obico Pro discount — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. A Chargebee failure other than \"not found\" cannot be produced without changing the\nserver's Chargebee settings.\n\n### JusPrin 1 plan read: any failure answers a 500 with \"Failed to retrieve subscription plan\" — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. It needs the database to fail during the read, which cannot be arranged from\noutside the code on the shared local stack.\n\n### A report photo in HEIC, or the server failing to read the upload — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. An operating-system read failure of an uploaded file cannot be caused from\noutside. A HEIC photo is already the person's and is already told so, which is right.\n"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"79f871b2-3241-4f53-b15d-bae491eab85c","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChQ91DVfMzqpGnk7tXCcV","requestRef":"514922ba-94ca-433c-a118-3117e7be5f05","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"ff38380e-7c1a-4074-b4fd-b29eb07bd101","timestamp":"2026-11-25T18:44:11.433Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","madeBy":"model","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/misrouted-failures","sessionId":"b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04","version":"2.1.330","gitBranch":"claude/misrouted-failures","slug":"linear-finding-kay-of-b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04","dataFlowOn":true}
{"type":"file-history-delta","messageId":"ff38380e-7c1a-4074-b4fd-b29eb07bd101","snapshotMessageId":"8d5554c4-8c53-4eac-b27a-8513dc276acd","trackingPath":"/Users/kenneth/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_cd3120f7-030a-4af8-a6d1-28ecae789f1a/misrouted-failures-simple-changes.md","backup":{"backupFileName":null,"version":1,"backupTime":"2026-11-25T18:44:11.452Z","realParentDir":"/Users/kenneth/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_cd3120f7-030a-4af8-a6d1-28ecae789f1a"},"timestamp":"2026-11-25T18:44:11.453Z"}
{"parentUuid":"ff38380e-7c1a-4074-b4fd-b29eb07bd101","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"72013b54-b18f-48cf-ab14-3227cd513d22","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TyYsmi8jGCx5X3kUnFasKk","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kenneth/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_cd3120f7-030a-4af8-a6d1-28ecae789f1a/misrouted-failures-simple-changes.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"48f6d077-3cc2-4359-b54e-4d8224701b4e","timestamp":"2026-11-25T18:44:11.482Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kenneth/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_cd3120f7-030a-4af8-a6d1-28ecae789f1a/misrouted-failures-simple-changes.md","content":"# The 17 simple changes to misrouted failures (2026-11-25)\n\nKenneth approved the 17 places that `misrouted-failures-simple-or-not.md` calls simple, each only after\nits failure has been made to happen (\"Yes, the 17\"). This file has one entry per place. Each changed\nplace says what happened before the change, the change itself, what happened after, which handler\nreports it, and whether a person can get there by doing something ordinary. Each place left untouched\nsays why.\n\n**Counts.** 6 places changed (3 in the app, 3 on the server). Two of them are changed only in part,\nbecause only some of their spots could be made to happen: billing, and the shipped printer profiles.\n11 places are left untouched (5 in the app, 6 on the server).\n\n**Where the work is.** Nothing is committed or pushed.\n- App: the worktree `/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/misrouted-failures`, branch\n  `claude/misrouted-failures`, uncommitted. Full diff: `misrouted-failures-simple-changes-app.patch` in\n  this folder.\n- Server: `/Users/kenneth/Projects/tsd-enterprise`, `web_ent/jusprin/`, uncommitted. Full diff:\n  `misrouted-failures-simple-changes-server.patch` in this folder.\n\n**How the failures were made to happen.**\n- App: a scratch copy of the built app with its own data folder. A local stand-in program answered in\n  place of Sentry and, where needed, in place of the AI service, and passed everything else to the\n  local server. Every Sentry report the app sent was counted there. Nothing went to a real Sentry\n  project, and nothing went to a real AI service.\n- Server: the local server over HTTP, signed in with a test account's token. The local server's\n  `SENTRY_DSN` is empty, so its 500s go nowhere.\n- Evidence folders (logs, page messages, the stand-in's received reports):\n  `/private/tmp/claude/claude-502/-Users-kenneth-Projects-JusPrin/b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04/scratchpad/live/runs/<name>-before` and `-after`.\n  The scratchpad can be cleared by a reboot.\n\n**Tests.**\n- Server: all of `jusprin.v2.tests` pass. Each new or changed test was also run on the old code and\n  fails there.\n- App: `agent_bridge_tests` (648 cases), `jusprin_profile_tests` (20) and `jusprin_tests` (200) all\n  pass.\n\n## The app\n\n### Sign out with no network — changed\n\n- **Before.** The local server was reached through a proxy, and the proxy was stopped before signing\n  out. The app was then sent the same message Preferences sends for \"Sign out\". The log said\n  \"Unexpected error while signing out of JusPrin: … revoking the token at …/o/revoke_token/ answered 0:\n  curl:Couldn't connect to server\", and 1 report reached the stand-in (run `signout-before`).\n- **Change** (`Agent/CloudSignInMachine.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n               .on_error(reported(\"signing out of JusPrin\", [url](std::string body, std::string error, unsigned status) {\n  +                // No answer, such as no network: the token is already\n  +                // forgotten on this computer, and nobody can act on it.\n  +                if (status == 0) {\n  +                    BOOST_LOG_TRIVIAL(warning) << \"Signing out of JusPrin reached no server at \" << url << \": \" << error;\n  +                    return;\n  +                }\n                   // Forgotten on this computer, the token still works on the\n                   // server.\n                   throw RuntimeError(\"revoking the token at \" + url + \" answered \" + std::to_string(status) + \": \" + error +\n  ```\n- **After.** Same arrangement. One warning line, \"Signing out of JusPrin reached no server at …\", and\n  0 reports (run `signout-after`). The token is still gone from the app's settings file.\n- **Handler.** Any other failure still throws inside `reported(\"signing out of JusPrin\", …)`, which\n  reports it.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes: signing out with the network off. That is why no answer at all is now not\n  reported. Nothing is shown, as before, because there is nothing for the person to do. An answer the\n  server refuses is still ours and still reported.\n\n### Own key: the service is down or overloaded — changed\n\nThe change covers three spots: an error answer of 500 or above, an overload or server error named\ninside a reply, and the AI models list. It applies only to a profile with the person's own key. It\ndoes not apply to a JusPrin account, where the service is ours, or to a model on this computer.\n\n- **Before.** The app used an OpenRouter profile with a key, and its AI address was pointed at the\n  stand-in.\n  - A 502 with OpenRouter's own words, \"Your chosen model is down…\" (run `40-down-before`): the chat\n    showed \"Something went wrong on our side\", and 2 reports were sent.\n  - A reply that failed inside the stream with `server_error` / `provider_overloaded` (run\n    `40-overloaded-in-reply-before`): the same sentence, and 2 reports.\n  - The chat title request failing with 502 after a good reply (run `40-title-down-before`): the log\n    said \"Unexpected error while making a chat's title\", and 1 report was sent.\n  - The models list answering 503 (run `40-models-down-before`): 4 reports for 4 failed reads, 2 of\n    JusPrin's list and 2 of OpenRouter's.\n- **Change** (`Agent/ProviderAgent.cpp`, `Agent/ModelCatalogFetch.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n       if (expected)\n           return this->expected(std::move(*expected), answered, words);\n  +    // The service of the person's own key failing on its side: whoever runs\n  +    // it can fix it, and nobody here can.\n  +    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in && !m_config.profile.on_this_computer && event.status >= 500)\n  +        // TRN %1% is an AI provider, such as OpenAI.\n  +        return this->expected({\"network_error\", Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The AI request could not reach the %1% service.\")), m_config.profile.display_name), true},\n  +                              answered, words);\n       unexpected(answered, words);\n  ```\n  ```diff\n   std::optional<AgentError> ProviderAgent::in_reply_answer(const json& answer) const\n   {\n  -    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in)\n  +    if (!m_config.profile.account_sign_in) {\n  +        // The service of the person's own key overloaded or failing on its\n  +        // side, as Anthropic, OpenAI and OpenRouter name it inside a reply.\n  +        const ServiceSays says = service_says(answer);\n  +        if (!m_config.profile.on_this_computer &&\n  +            (says.type == \"overloaded_error\" || says.type == \"api_error\" || says.code == \"server_error\" ||\n  +             says.error_type == \"provider_overloaded\" || says.error_type == \"provider_unavailable\" || says.error_type == \"server\"))\n  +            // TRN %1% is an AI provider, such as OpenAI.\n  +            return AgentError{\"network_error\", Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The AI request could not reach the %1% service.\")), m_config.profile.display_name), true};\n           return provider_answer(0, answer);\n  +    }\n  ```\n  ```diff\n               fetch->done(std::move(fetch->result));\n  +            // The service of the person's own key failing on its side is for\n  +            // whoever runs it; the list is marked failed above.\n  +            if (!fetch->profile.account_sign_in && !fetch->profile.on_this_computer && status >= 500)\n  +                return;\n               if (const std::optional<std::string> fault = model_list_fault(status, body))\n                   throw std::runtime_error(*fault);\n  ```\n  The sentence is the one the app already shows when the service cannot be reached. It is an existing\n  msgid, so the catalogs do not change. The in-reply names come from each service's documentation:\n  Anthropic's `overloaded_error` and `api_error`, OpenAI's `server_error`, and OpenRouter's\n  `provider_overloaded`, `provider_unavailable` and `server`.\n- **After.** Same arrangements.\n  - The 502 and the in-reply failure: \"The AI request could not reach the OpenRouter service.\", with\n    the retry the app offers for that sentence, and 0 reports.\n  - The title: the chat stays untitled, and 0 reports.\n  - The models list: 2 reports, both for JusPrin's list, whose failure is ours. OpenRouter's 2 are\n    gone.\n- **Handler.** An error answer below 500 that names nothing the person can act on still goes to\n  `unexpected`, which throws. The throw ends in `AgentHost::pump_stream`, and `AgentWebView` reports\n  it. A JusPrin account's failures are unchanged and still reported. A models-list fault still throws\n  inside `reported(\"reading the AI models list\", …)`.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes, a person with their own key meets this whenever their service has an\n  outage. Nobody here can fix it, so it is not reported. The sentence the app already has tells them\n  the trouble is at that service.\n- **A repeating failure.** Each retry is one more answer of 500 or above, and none of them is reported\n  now. Before the change, each retry sent another report.\n- **Tests.** `tests/agent/test_provider_agent.cpp`: 500, 503 and 529 moved from the \"names nothing a\n  person can act on\" loop into the table of expected failures, as \"could not reach the Test Provider\n  service\". `tests/agent/test_anthropic_codec.cpp`: an in-reply `overloaded_error` now expects the\n  `network_error` failure, not a throw. These were changed tests, and the old assertions passed on the\n  old code, which is how I know the new ones fail on it. I did not run the new tests on an old build.\n\n### Printer profiles shipped with the app fail to install or read — changed in part\n\nThe place has three spots. Two are in `PrinterSetup/OrcaImporter.cpp`. They are left untouched\nbecause they are reached only by importing from an OrcaSlicer install, and I did not make them happen.\nThe third, the printer setup form, is changed.\n\n- **Before.** I made a scratch copy of the app and cut Creality's\n  `resources/profiles/Creality/filament/fdm_filament_common.json` to 200 bytes. On Home I opened the\n  printer column's add-printer chat, chose to set up a printer myself, and picked the Creality\n  Ender-3 V3 SE with a 0.4 nozzle (run `profiles-before`). The form said \"The printer profiles could\n  not be read: Failed loading configuration file …\", followed by the file's full path on this\n  computer. 0 reports were sent.\n- **Change** (`PrinterSetup/SetupOptionsLoader.cpp`):\n  ```diff\n       SetupOptions options;\n       PresetBundle bundle;\n  -    // A vendor that cannot be read is named, not thrown; its error is in the text.\n  +    // The profiles shipped with the app fail to read only on a broken\n  +    // install, which is ours. The loader logs each file's error, with its path.\n       std::vector<std::string> failed;\n  -    const std::string errors =\n  -        bundle.load_system_vendor_from_dir(profiles_directory, vendor_id, ForwardCompatibilitySubstitutionRule::EnableSilent, &failed).second;\n  -    if (!failed.empty()) {\n  -        // TRN %1% is the profile loader's own error, in English.\n  -        options.error = Slic3r::format(translated(L(\"The printer profiles could not be read: %1%\")), errors);\n  -        return options;\n  -    }\n  +    bundle.load_system_vendor_from_dir(profiles_directory, vendor_id, ForwardCompatibilitySubstitutionRule::EnableSilent, &failed);\n  +    if (!failed.empty())\n  +        throw std::runtime_error(\"The shipped printer profiles of \" + vendor_id + \" could not be read\");\n  ```\n  The includes of `libslic3r/format.hpp` and `Translate.hpp` were removed with it, because nothing else\n  in the file used them.\n- **After.** Same broken copy, same steps (run `profiles-after`). 1 report: \"Unexpected error while\n  running the printer chat: std::runtime_error: The shipped printer profiles of Creality could not be\n  read\". It has no path in it. The log has one \"Unexpected error\" line, and the form shows no error\n  text of its own.\n- **Handler.** The printer panel's `reported(\"running the printer chat\", …)`.\n- **Ordinary person?** No. The person cannot change the files shipped inside the app, and a good\n  install reads them. Only a broken install, which is ours, reaches this.\n- **Test.** `tests/jusprin/test_setup_options.cpp`: a folder without the profiles now must throw\n  \"shipped printer profiles of Prusa\". It failed before the change because nothing threw.\n- **Follow-up, not done.** `SetupOptions::error` and the form's handling of it are now never set. Removing\n  them is a further deletion outside this place.\n\n### Password sign-in: any 400 is \"That password is wrong.\" — untouched\n\nI could not make a 400 that is ours happen. The server's password sign-in answered every attempt with\na 400 that is the person's. A password of only spaces got `required`, and a wrong password got the\ninvalid-password code. No answer from it reached the app's \"anything else\" branch.\n\n### JusPrin's model list — untouched\n\nThe local server's models list answers only 200, 401, 426 or 500, and it has no rate limit. The answer\nthe place is about cannot be produced without changing the server.\n\n### The local tool server's helper tells the outside AI client to open JusPrin when JusPrin is open — untouched\n\nThe failure needs the tool protocol to change between the helper and the app. The protocol has not\nchanged, so it could not be made to happen.\n\n### A damaged printer-facts file is emptied with no word — untouched\n\nThe handler check failed. One path to the file's read starts from `EVT_SLICE_STATUS_CHANGED`, and the\nnearest handler on that path is in an OrcaSlicer-owned file. By the rules of this task, the place is\nleft alone.\n\n### A printer-host connection test that throws — untouched\n\nNo host's connection test could be made to throw against the real hosts. The Moonraker and OctoPrint\nstand-ins that run in Docker each answered with a failure value, not a throw.\n\n## The server\n\n### The app sends something the server refuses — changed\n\nThe gateway's refusals, the print report's two refusals, and the web link's two refusals now raise,\nso Django answers 500 and reports it. Kenneth on 2026-11-15: \"Treat them as unexpected errors.\"\n\n- **Before** (local server over HTTP, with the test account's token):\n  - `POST /ent/jusprin/v2/responses` with the model `no-such-model` answered 400 `unknown_model` \"This\n    model is not available.\". The log had one info line ending \"failure: answered 400 unknown_model\".\n  - `POST print_reports` with an empty `gcode_hash`, or with `facts={`, answered 400 `invalid_request`.\n  - `POST web_link` with the body `{` or `[1]` answered 400 `invalid_request`, with a \"Bad Request\"\n    warning in the log.\n- **Change**:\n  ```diff\n           except GatewayError as e:\n  +            # A request the app sent that this server refuses is ours: the app\n  +            # is on the other end. Kenneth, 2026-11-15: \"Treat them as\n  +            # unexpected errors.\"\n  +            if e.code in ('invalid_request', 'unknown_model'):\n  +                raise\n               # The code only, never the message or details, which can hold the\n               # person's numbers.\n               facts['failure'] = f'answered {e.status} {e.code}'\n  ```\n  ```diff\n  -    try:\n  -        facts = json.loads(request.POST.get('facts', '{}'))\n  -    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +    # The app fills these in, so a report it sent that is refused here is ours.\n  +    facts = json.loads(request.POST.get('facts', '{}'))\n       if not gcode_hash or outcome not in dict(PrintReport.OUTCOMES) or not isinstance(facts, dict):\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +        raise ValueError(f'The app sent a print report with gcode_hash {gcode_hash!r}, outcome {outcome!r} '\n  +                         f'and facts of type {type(facts).__name__}')\n  ```\n  ```diff\n  -    try:\n  -        body = json.loads(request.body or b'{}')\n  -    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +    # The app writes this body, so one refused here is ours.\n  +    body = json.loads(request.body or b'{}')\n       if not isinstance(body, dict):\n  -        return _refuse('invalid_request', 400)\n  +        raise ValueError(f'The app asked for a web link with a body of type {type(body).__name__}')\n  ```\n- **After.** Same requests.\n  - The gateway answered 500 `server_error` \"Something went wrong on our side\", and logged an error\n    with the traceback ending `GatewayError: unknown_model`.\n  - The print report answered 500, and the log shows \"ValueError: The app sent a print report with\n    gcode_hash '', outcome 'success' and facts of type dict\".\n  - The web link answered 500 with a `JSONDecodeError` for `{`, and \"ValueError: The app asked for a\n    web link with a body of type list\" for `[1]`. A good body still answered 200.\n- **Handler.** The gateway's `_handle`, which calls `capture_exception` and answers `server_error`.\n  For the two views it is Django's own error handling and `handler500`.\n- **Ordinary person?** No. The app writes every one of these requests. The model names come from the\n  list the server itself sends. The app already treats the server's error answers as ours, so what the\n  person sees does not change.\n- **Tests.** In `test_gateway.py` and `test_capture.py`, 12 assertions now expect 500 `server_error`,\n  and the 8 tests named `…_is_400` are renamed `…_is_ours`. `test_print_reports.py` and\n  `test_web_link.py` each have one test that now expects the error to be raised. All of them fail on\n  the old code.\n\n### Guesses in billing — changed in part\n\nThe place has three spots. Only the card lookup is changed.\n\n- **Card lookup.**\n  - **Before.** A Chargebee failure of any kind showed \"no card on file\". A \"not found\" from the real\n    test site, read through the server's own code, gave no card, as it should. A refusal of another\n    kind could not be produced against Chargebee without changing the site's keys. So the refusal was\n    made with Django's test client inside a rolled-back test, not over a socket. On the old code the\n    page still loaded, with no card shown.\n  - **Change** (`ledger/billing.py`):\n    ```diff\n         try:\n             card = chargebee.Customer.retrieve(customer_id(user), env=_site()).card\n    -    except chargebee.APIError:\n    +    except chargebee.InvalidRequestError as e:\n    +        # An account that paid without a customer on this site, such as a\n    +        # plan bought before it had its own, has no card here. Anything else\n    +        # Chargebee answers is ours.\n    +        if getattr(e, 'api_error_code', None) != 'resource_not_found':\n    +            raise\n             return None\n    ```\n  - **After.** \"Not found\" still gives no card. A 401 `api_authentication_failed` now raises out of\n    the Billing and usage page.\n  - **Handler.** Django's `handler500`.\n  - **Ordinary person?** Not finding the customer is ordinary for an account with no customer on\n    JusPrin 2's site, and it still means no card. Nothing else a person can do reaches the other\n    failures.\n  - **Tests** (`test_billing.py`): a new test, where a 401 from Chargebee must make the page raise. It\n    fails on the old code with \"APIError not raised\". The cancel test's last page load now patches the\n    card lookup, because it used to rely on the old catch-all.\n- **Account deletion with \"subscription not found\" — untouched.** No JusPrin 2 Chargebee key exists\n  locally, so the deletion's Chargebee call cannot be made against a real site.\n- **A report conflict in the database — untouched.** To produce it, a report would have to be stored,\n  and a report's photo goes to the shared store. That would leave a stray file on a shared system.\n\n### The browser session is gone at checkout, or on the way back from paying — changed\n\n- **Before.** Over HTTP on the local server, I signed in to Credits and plans with the app's link, read\n  the page's form token, and then opened the guest page, which signs the browser out. Pressing the\n  pack's button answered 302 to Credits and plans. Coming back from paying, at\n  `checkout/done?id=…`, also answered 302 to Credits and plans, with nothing said.\n- **Change** (`v2/web.py`):\n  ```diff\n       user = _user(request)\n       if user is None:\n  -        return HttpResponseRedirect(reverse('jusprin_v2_web_prices'))\n  +        # A tab left open past its sign-in: the person's. They open the page\n  +        # from JusPrin again, as for a link that expired.\n  +        logging.getLogger(__name__).info('JusPrin 2 checkout pressed in a browser no longer signed in')\n  +        return render(request, 'jusprin/v2/sign_in_result.html', {\n  +            'heading': _('This link has expired'),\n  +            'line': _('Go back to JusPrin and open the page again.'),\n  +        }, status=410)\n  ```\n  ```diff\n       user = _user(request)\n  -    bought = billing.finish_checkout(user, request.GET['id']) if user and request.GET.get('id') else None\n  +    if user is None:\n  +        # Back from paying in a browser no longer signed in: the person's. The\n  +        # webhook adds what they bought, which they see in JusPrin.\n  +        logging.getLogger(__name__).info('JusPrin 2 checkout came back to a browser no longer signed in')\n  +        return render(request, 'jusprin/v2/sign_in_result.html', {\n  +            'heading': _('This link has expired'),\n  +            'line': _('Go back to JusPrin and open the page again.'),\n  +        }, status=410)\n  +    bought = billing.finish_checkout(user, request.GET['id']) if request.GET.get('id') else None\n  ```\n  The page and both lines already exist and are used by the expired sign-in link, so there are no new\n  strings.\n- **After.** Same steps. Both answered 410 with the page \"This link has expired. Go back to JusPrin and\n  open the page again.\", and each logged its info line.\n- **Handler.** None needed. The failure is the person's, so it is handled with normal control flow and\n  not reported.\n- **Ordinary person?** Yes: a tab left open past its sign-in, or signing out in another tab. They are\n  now told to open the page from JusPrin again.\n- **Test** (`test_billing.py`): a new test, where both addresses with no signed-in browser must give\n  410 and \"This link has expired\". It fails on the old code with \"302 != 410\".\n\n### A paid Chargebee event that matches nothing — untouched\n\nThe \"ordinary person\" check found that a person can get there. Deleting an account (`_delete_account`)\nleaves Chargebee's later events for that customer matching nothing. Raising there would report an\nordinary deletion, and Chargebee would retry the event again and again.\n\n### A wrong-scope or wrong-client token is told \"Sign in to JusPrin to continue.\" — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. The only tokens are the JusPrin client's \"read write\" tokens. Making a token for\nanother client or another scope would write to the shared database.\n\n### JusPrin 1: a cancelled checkout raises a 500 — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. JusPrin 1's checkout sets no cancel address, so cancelling never reaches this\ncallback.\n\n### JusPrin 1 checkout: a bare catch drops the Obico Pro discount — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. A Chargebee failure other than \"not found\" cannot be produced without changing the\nserver's Chargebee settings.\n\n### JusPrin 1 plan read: any failure answers a 500 with \"Failed to retrieve subscription plan\" — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. It needs the database to fail during the read, which cannot be arranged from\noutside the code on the shared local stack.\n\n### A report photo in HEIC, or the server failing to read the upload — untouched\n\nNot made to happen. An operating-system read failure of an uploaded file cannot be caused from\noutside. A HEIC photo is already the person's and is already told so, which is right.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ff38380e-7c1a-4074-b4fd-b29eb07bd101","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/misrouted-failures","sessionId":"b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04","version":"2.1.330","gitBranch":"claude/misrouted-failures","slug":"linear-finding-kay-of-b575a9e5-080c-46d7-b71a-f5842e099a04","dataFlowOn":true}
